  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1256 PM CDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nAt the synoptic level, the region remains under the weakening \ninfluence of H5 ridging and surface high pressure across the \nUpper Ohio Valley. Another mid- level ridge is located across \nthe Desert Southwest, while broad troughing is present across \nthe northern and central Great Plains. A deep 992 mb surface low\nis located over Hudson Bay, with a weak/diffuse trailing cold \nfront oriented from NE to SW across the Great Lakes and Central \nGreat Plains. \n\nThe surface high over the Upper Ohio Valley will bring \ncontinued dry and seasonable temperature and humidity values\nthrough this afternoon. Tonight, showers and thunderstorms will\ndevelop to our west across the the Lower Missouri Valley as a\nshortwave trough interacts with the frontal boundary. Some of\nthis activity (mainly scattered showers and a few rumbles of\nthunder) will move into southeast MO and southern IL Thursday\nmorning before weakening. During the rest of the day into the\nevening hours on Thursday, mainly isolated diurnal convection\nwill be possible. \n\nAdditional shortwaves will trigger more rounds of scattered\nconvection very late Thursday night into Friday morning and \nagain Friday afternoon into Saturday morning (with lingering \nactivity into Saturday afternoon in west KY). We have been \nmessaging a possibly low-end severe risk with this activity, \nbut the latest CAMs are less impressive with the already meager \nshear values. Latest thinking is this activity will be more \ngarden- variety summertime storms more than anything else. \n\nBy Sunday into next week, the frontal boundary will retreat to\nthe north as H5 ridging across the Desert Southwest expands\neastward into the central/southern Great Plains and Ozarks. This\nwill mean increasing temperature and humidity levels, beginning\nSunday and continuing into early next week. Right now, high\ntemperatures are forecast to reach the lower 90s with dew point\ntemperatures in the middle to upper 70s. This will yield daily \nheat index values of 100-105 degrees Sunday through at least\nTuesday next week.",
